1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,150
Listen to a short talk about Astronomy.

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:06,110
In ancient times, many people believed the earth was a flat disc.

3
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:07,703
Well over 2000 years ago,

4
00:00:07,703 --> 00:00:13,120
the ancient Greek philosophers were able to put forward two good arguments proving that it was not.

5
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:18,060
Direct observations of heavenly bodies was the basis of both these arguments.

6
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:20,940
First, the Greeks knew that during eclipses of the moon

7
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:23,317
the earth was between the sun and the moon,

8
00:00:23,317 --> 00:00:25,340
and they saw that during these eclipses

9
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:28,020
the earth's shadow on the moon was always round.

10
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:31,090
They realized this could be true only if the earth was spherical.

11
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:36,390
If the earth were a flat disc, then its shadow during eclipses would not be a perfect circle,

12
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:39,040
it would be stretched out into a long ellipse.

13
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:43,050
The second argument was based on what the Greeks saw during their travels.

14
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:45,320
They noticed that the North Star, or Polaris

15
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,509
appeared lower in the sky when they traveled south,

16
00:00:47,509 --> 00:00:52,755
in more northerly regions, the North Star appeared to them to be much higher in the sky. By the way,

17
00:00:52,755 --> 00:01:00,100
it was also from this difference in the apparent position of the North Star that the Greeks first calculated the approximate distance around the circumference of the earth.

18
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:04,200
A figure recorded in ancient documents says 400000 stadia

19
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:06,060
that's the plural of the word stadium.

20
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:09,449
Today, it's not known exactly what length one stadium represents,

21
00:01:09,449 --> 00:01:14,070
but let's say it was about 200 meters, the length of many athletic stadiums.

22
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:17,732
This would make the Greek's estimate about twice the figure accepted today,

23
00:01:17,732 --> 00:01:19,820
a very good estimate for those writing

24
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:22,240
so long before even the first telescope was invented.
